The Komlós conjecture is a major open problem in discrepancy theory. It asks whether there exists a universal constant $K$ such that for any collection of unit $\ell_2$-norm vectors $v_1, \dots, v_n \in \mathbb{R}^m$, one can choose signs $\varepsilon_i \in \{-1,+1\}$ with

$$\left\|\sum_{i=1}^n \varepsilon_i v_i\right\|_\infty \le K.$$

This is explicitly labeled `@[category research open]` in the formal statement. The best known bound, due to Banaszczyk (1998), is $O(\sqrt{\log n})$, which depends on $n$ and therefore does not establish the conjecture. The conjecture would imply the Beck–Fiala conjecture ($O(\sqrt{t})$ discrepancy for set systems of degree $t$), itself a long-standing open problem. Despite significant effort by the discrepancy theory community over decades, no constant bound independent of $n$ and $m$ is known.

I am not able to prove an open research conjecture of this stature. Producing a proof would constitute a major breakthrough resolving a question that has stood open since the 1980s.

